Store coordinates as GeoJSON objects, NOT as bare arrays:
// Point — for cities, sensors, devices
{
_id: "p1",
name: "London Bridge",
location: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-0.0877, 51.5079] }
// ^lng ^lat
}
Coordinate order: [longitude, latitude]. Not [lat, lng]. This is the #1 mistake — GeoJSON uses lng-first; Google Maps uses lat-first in its URLs. Get it backward and your London point lands in the Indian Ocean, off the coast of Somalia.
Polygon — closed area:
{
_id: "z1",
name: "Central London zone",
area: {
type: "Polygon",
coordinates: [[
[-0.13, 51.50],
[-0.08, 51.50],
[-0.08, 51.52],
[-0.13, 51.52],
[-0.13, 51.50] // first + last must match (closed ring)
]]
}
}
Other types: LineString, MultiPoint, MultiLineString, MultiPolygon, GeometryCollection. Each has a defined coordinates shape per the GeoJSON spec.

db.places.find({
location: {
$near: {
$geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-0.1, 51.5] },
$minDistance: 0, // metres (Point + 2dsphere)
$maxDistance: 5000 // 5 km
}
}
})
Returns documents sorted by distance ascending. $near requires a 2dsphere index.
For legacy 2d indexes (planar), $near returns distance in radians; that's why most teams ditched 2d.
$nearSphere is the legacy alias when working with 2d indexes for spherical results. With 2dsphere both work identically.
Limitations:
Can't combine $near with $or.
Can't use $near inside aggregation $match — use $geoNear (Chapter 6).
Implicit sort by distance — can't override.

// inside a polygon
db.places.find({
location: {
$geoWithin: {
$geometry: { type: "Polygon", coordinates: [[ ... ]] }
}
}
})
// inside a circle (centre + radius in radians — use earth's radius / metres)
const EARTH_RADIUS_METRES = 6_378_100;
db.places.find({
location: {
$geoWithin: {
$centerSphere: [[-0.1, 51.5], 5000 / EARTH_RADIUS_METRES]
}
}
})
// inside a box (legacy planar)
db.places.find({
location: { $geoWithin: { $box: [[-0.13, 51.50], [-0.08, 51.52]] } }
})
$geoWithin doesn't sort. Faster than $near when you only need set membership.
$centerSphere radius is in radians — divide metres by Earth's radius (≈ 6,378,100 m) to convert.
Trick: for "all delivery couriers within 5 km of this address — order doesn't matter for the dispatcher's filter", use $geoWithin: { $centerSphere: [...] }. It's noticeably faster than $near.

// find polygons that intersect a line (e.g., flood plain crossing roads)
db.flood_zones.find({
area: {
$geoIntersects: {
$geometry: { type: "LineString", coordinates: [[lng1, lat1], [lng2, lat2]] }
}
}
})
Pairs of geometries that touch or overlap — useful for "does this delivery route pass through any blocked zone?".

When a device sends { location: {Point} }:
const fences = await db.geofences.find({
area: { $geoIntersects: { $geometry: device.location } }
}).toArray();
const previousFenceIds = await getDeviceFenceState(device.id);
const currentFenceIds = fences.map(f => f._id);
for (const newFence of currentFenceIds.filter(id => !previousFenceIds.includes(id))) {
emit('fence.enter', { device: device.id, fence: newFence });
}
for (const oldFence of previousFenceIds.filter(id => !currentFenceIds.includes(id))) {
emit('fence.exit', { device: device.id, fence: oldFence });
}
await setDeviceFenceState(device.id, currentFenceIds);
Index geofences.area as 2dsphere; the $geoIntersects query is fast.

$near returns sorted ascending — that's the easy case.
For "page 2 of nearest 20", .skip(20).limit(20) works but pays the skip cost (Post 02). For huge result sets:
// page 1
const page1 = await db.places.aggregate([
{ $geoNear: { near: pt, distanceField: "dist_m", spherical: true, maxDistance: 50000 } },
{ $sort: { dist_m: 1, _id: 1 } },
{ $limit: 20 }
]).toArray();
const last = page1[page1.length - 1];
// page 2 — cursor-based
const page2 = await db.places.aggregate([
{ $geoNear: { near: pt, distanceField: "dist_m", spherical: true, maxDistance: 50000 } },
{ $match: { $or: [
{ dist_m: { $gt: last.dist_m } },
{ dist_m: last.dist_m, _id: { $gt: last._id } }
]}},
{ $sort: { dist_m: 1, _id: 1 } },
{ $limit: 20 }
]).toArray();
Range pagination on (distance, _id). Scales.

// couriers — current location + zone tag
db.createCollection("couriers")
db.couriers.createIndex({ status: 1, current_location: "2dsphere" })
// zones — geofences for cities / districts
db.createCollection("zones")
db.zones.createIndex({ area: "2dsphere" })
// dispatching nearest available courier
const candidates = await db.couriers.aggregate([
{ $geoNear: {
near: drop_point,
distanceField: "dist_m",
spherical: true,
maxDistance: 10000,
query: { status: "available", capacity: { $gt: 0 } }
}},
{ $limit: 5 }
]).toArray();
Geofence on pickup: courier's location enters the customer's pickup zone → emit pickup.imminent event.
Heat map of demand: $bucket on $geoNear results to produce a histogram of pending orders per distance bin from the depot.
All powered by one 2dsphere index per relevant collection.

What · How
Index · { field: "2dsphere" }
Point · { type: "Point", coordinates: [lng, lat] }
Polygon · { type: "Polygon", coordinates: [[ [lng,lat], ..., [first lng,lat] ]] }
Nearest sorted · $near (find)
Nearest in aggregation · $geoNear (first stage)
Inside polygon · $geoWithin: { $geometry: polygon }
Inside circle · $geoWithin: { $centerSphere: [centre, radius/EARTH_R] }
Intersects · $geoIntersects: { $geometry: ... }
Distance in metres · use 2dsphere; result in metres
Coordinate order · lng, lat — not lat, lng
Polygon closure · first = last coord
Antimeridian · split polygons crossing ±180
Geofence · poll $geoIntersects per update or change-stream-driven
Pagination by distance · cursor on (dist, _id)
Sharding · region prefix → in-region queries hit one shard
Helper libs · @turf/turf, shapely, H3
